<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Dickens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Dickens was the most popular English novelist of the Victorian era. Works such as Oliver Twist, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-christmas-carol">A Christmas Carol</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Copperfield, Bleak House and Great Expectations, many first published in monthly instalments, combined comedy and memorable characters with sharp criticism of poverty, child labour, the workhouse and the law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-dickens.jpg" alt="Charles Dickens" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeremiah Gurney · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dickens_Gurney_head.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Writers including Jane Austen, <b>Charles Dickens</b>, the Brontë sisters, George Eliot, W.B. Yeats and James Joyce made the period a high point of literature in English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A Christmas Carol, by <b>Charles Dickens</b>, was published in London on 19 December 1843.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Dickens</b> published A Christmas Carol on 19 December 1843; the first edition sold out by Christmas Eve, and the book has never been out of print.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>It appears in <b>Charles Dickens</b>'s A Tale of Two Cities, Baroness Orczy's The Scarlet Pimpernel and Gertrud von Le Fort's The Song at the Scaffold, is sung in Umberto Giordano's opera Andrea Chénier and Eugene O'Neill's The Iceman Cometh, and Victor Hugo set it against &quot;La Marseillaise&quot; in…</blockquote><a href="/songs/carmagnole">La Carmagnole</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Dickens</b> refers to it in A Christmas Carol (1843), changing &quot;rest&quot; to &quot;bless&quot;, and George Eliot quotes it in Silas Marner (1861).</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-rest-ye-merry-gentlemen">God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Dickens</b>'s novella about the miser Ebenezer Scrooge went on sale and sold out its first printing by Christmas Eve.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-19">19 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Landport, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Gads Hill Place, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.8,-1.09&amp;year=1849" aria-label="Landport on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4525 401 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles Dickens"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4975 671h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4975 671h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.8,-1.09&amp;year=1849">What was here in 1849? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It was the best of times, it was the worst of times, it was the age of wisdom, it was the age of foolishness…&#8221;</p><footer>A Tale of Two Cities (1859), opening &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/98/pg98-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Annual income twenty pounds, annual expenditure nineteen nineteen and six, result happiness. Annual income twenty pounds, annual expenditure twenty pounds ought and six, result misery.&#8221;</p><footer>David Copperfield (1850) &#183; original &#183; spoken by Mr Micawber &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/766/pg766-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future.&#8221;</p><footer>A Christmas Carol (1843) &#183; original &#183; spoken by Scrooge &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46/pg46-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now, what I want is, Facts. Teach these boys and girls nothing but Facts. Facts alone are wanted in life.&#8221;</p><footer>Hard Times (1854), opening &#183; original &#183; spoken by Mr Gradgrind &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/786/pg786-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Heaven knows we need never be ashamed of our tears, for they are rain upon the blinding dust of earth, overlying our hard hearts.&#8221;</p><footer>Great Expectations (1861), ch. 19 &#183; original &#183; spoken by the narrator, Pip &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1400/pg1400-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is a far, far better thing that I do, than I have ever done; it is a far, far better rest that I go to than I have ever known.&#8221;</p><footer>A Tale of Two Cities (1859), closing words &#183; original &#183; the thoughts the narrator gives Sydney Carton at the guillotine &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/98/pg98-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
